00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
your mission critical applications  depend on correct data  that's why developers rely on relational  databases which ensure  data consistency however last  generations databases weren't designed  to handle today's  scale and availability requirements in  today's episode  we're talking about how spanner provides  the highest level of consistency of  any distributed relational database at  unlimited scale  consistency is one of the main reasons  to choose a relational database to back  your applications  strong consistency guarantees ensure  that data is correct regardless of how  it's accessed or updated  let's say you have a banking system that  transfers money between accounts  consistency ensures that the debit from  one account and the credit into the  other either both succeed or both  fail this simplifies your applications  and lets your dbas sleep well at night  when it comes time to scale however  traditional relational databases require  you to give up many of the consistency  protections that made them attractive in  the first place  this is because they rely on techniques  like sharding to divide the data across  multiple instances the overhead required  to manage these shards  typically forces operators to choose  between performance or consistency  in addition highly available systems  need to maintain  multiple copies of the data in case one  of the shards goes down  for many applications uncertainty in the  form of replication lag  or eventual consistency is a recipe for  complicated code  and tricky bugs this is where cloud  spanner can help  spanner is the only distributed  relational database that provides  external consistency the highest  consistency guarantee  this means that all changes in the  database appear from the outside as if  they happen sequentially  this dramatically simplifies a  developer's view of the data  no more sharded queries timing bugs or  compensating logic  to your application spanner looks like a  familiar single instance database  it handles the complexities of ensuring  consistent transactions  even as infrastructure scales up or  automatically fails over  spanner is fully managed so your  operators don't ever have to worry about  sharding or replication  so how does spanner provide external  consistency without sacrificing  availability or performance at scale  let's start by imagining your database  resides on a single machine  in this case it could use a simple  counter to order incoming transactions  however a single machine can only scale  up so far  beyond that you'll need to distribute  the work across many machines  keeping a single counter would be a  bottleneck between hundreds or thousands  of servers  spanner avoids this by allowing each  machine or node  to keep its own counter using a clock  but not just any clock using a  combination of gps  atomic clocks and google's dedicated  fiber optic network  spanner's truetime technology ensures  accurate ordering of transactions  without expensive centralized  coordination  truetime simplifies the work that  spanner has to do to provide strict  external consistency  this streamlining translates to better  performance and higher availability for  strong acid transactions  let's take a closer look at how spanner  manages the data involved in those  transactions  spanner partitions each table's data  into independent chunks  called splits each split is assigned  internally to a server unlike  traditional sharding  these splits are completely transparent  to downstream users  spanner automatically optimizes its  split configurations to balance out  workloads  and avoid hot spots when a split is  under heavy load  for example when updating the inventory  of a popular product  spanner resizes and rebalances the  affected splits to avoid overloading  individual servers splitting and  rebalancing happen completely behind the  scenes  dynamically adjusting based on your data  and workload  to avoid a single point of failure each  split in a regional instance is  replicated across  three isolated zones spanner uses the  well-known paxos consensus protocol to  ensure that everything stays in sync  even as the storage and machines change  underneath  during a write spanner first determines  which split  or splits will be affected each split  has a leader among its copies that  coordinates locking and replication  the leader assigns a timestamp to the  transaction using truetime  and tells the replicas about the updates  using paxos  once a majority of the replicas  acknowledge the update the leader  directs them to apply the changes to the  data at the agreed-upon timestamp  leadership updates dynamically within an  instance again to avoid single points of  failure  for a query that doesn't make any  updates spanner avoids the extra  overhead of taking locks  accessing an always consistent copy of  the data at the most recent time stamp  combined these optimizations ensure  consistency  and availability at scale spanner takes  distributed consistency a step further  with multi-region configurations  multi-region instances replicate data  over two or three separate geographic  locations  for example the us europe and asia  this added replication up to nine times  provides  industry-leading five nines of  availability  but doesn't all of this come at a  significant cost to performance  while the underlying work that spanner  does to ensure consistency and  availability is not free  you only pay a performance cost for what  you actually use  in other words if a query can run  without locking  it will or if a write only touches one  split it will bypass a more expensive  two-phase commit  this means that your applications will  continue to perform as they scale  with all the same consistency guarantees  by partitioning the data into splits  spanner can grow beyond a single machine  and resolve queries in parallel  improving scalability and performance  by replicating splits over multiple  zones and regions  connected by google's dedicated network  spanner is able to remain  highly available up to 99.999 percent  even under the most grueling workloads  finally by coordinating updates using  truetime and paxos  spanner ensures that your applications  only ever see a consistent view of the  data  the best part all of this is handled  transparently for you behind the scenes  so there you have it the magic sauce  behind spanner's consistency  at scale thank you for joining us today  for this episode of spanner  database unlimited  you

